    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "nc_description",
      "content": "## About Noise Criterion (NC)\n\nNoise Criterion (NC) levels were established in the U.S. to rate indoor noise from air-conditioning equipment and similar sources. In Europe, the alternative Noise Rating Curve (NR) is commonly used.\n\n### How NC Works\n\nThe NC method is based on measurements of sound pressure levels across octave bands ranging from 63 to 8000 Hz. A set of NC criteria curves defines the limits that must not be exceeded to meet occupant acceptance in specific spaces.\n\nThe NC rating is determined by:\n1. Plotting measured sound pressure at each octave band\n2. Finding the lowest NC curve not exceeded by the measured spectrum\n3. Assigning that NC curve number as the rating\n\n### Example\n\nFor measured sound pressure levels:\n- 62.5 Hz: 40 dB\n- 125 Hz: 50 dB\n- 250 Hz: 55 dB\n- 500 Hz: 60 dB\n- 1000 Hz: 50 dB\n- 2000 Hz: 55 dB\n- 4000 Hz: 45 dB\n- 8000 Hz: 45 dB\n\nThe resulting Noise Criterion rating would be approximately NC-57 dB.\n\n### NCB Curves\n\nBalanced Noise Criteria (NCB) curves are a newer standard used to specify acceptable background noise levels in occupied spaces, including noise from air-conditioning systems and other ambient noise sources. They are intended to replace the traditional NC curves."
